The [original incident](/kb/incidents/4471) says one known issue and one queued fix.
Its rendered evidence separates a shared-parser failure from a booking-form hint failure.
Keep a ticket for each cause; a parser fix does not establish that the form hint is fixed.

## Engineering outcome: closed 5 October

The course desk's engineering update closes #4471 on 5 October 2026 with two different outcomes:

- [Parser ticket](https://github.com/Olszi37/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1): **fixed**,
  `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. Close as completed on the strength of that update.
- [Hint ticket](https://github.com/Olszi37/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2): **won't fix**,
  because the booking-v3 form was retired. Close as not planned; retirement is not a hint fix.

The counts and chart below preserve the earlier export. Their historical queue labels do
not override the later engineering update. The five anomalous reports still lack a proven
cause; closing the parent tickets does not prove that each symptom was fixed.

## Count the reports, not export rows or intake guesses

The following view deduplicates by report ID, selects reports merged into #4471,
and resolves the tenant directory before classifying. It computes its counts when rendered.
This is the desk's export dated 4 October 2026, not a live queue total.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The classifier uses the tenant's locale and booking-form version plus the report's channel.
It asks the platform whether that locale is day-first. Browser locale and intake tags are
intake guesses; neither establishes the cause. Overlapping export pages repeat some IDs;
one ID is one report. See `evidence/audit.json` for the assigned IDs and reconciliation.

## Two separate fixes

- **Parser:** the shared parser reads dates month-first for day-first tenants. App and email
  reports, and forms without the booking-v3 hint mismatch, fall into this group. Parse with
  the tenant's declared format. Verify ambiguous dates and the relevant entry routes.
- **Hint:** booking-v3 tells a day-first tenant to type `MM/DD/YYYY`, while the form reads
  the date in the tenant's order. Derive the hint and input interpretation from one format
  rule, then verify that following the hint preserves the intended day and month.

## Five reports still need investigation

The table's footnote preserves the desk's one-day-move reports under their tenant-derived
parser classification. R-41202, R-41209, R-41236, R-41344 and R-41620 describe a day shift,
regional disagreement, or an invite arriving before the booking. Those descriptions do
not establish a parser failure. Inspect the original timestamps, zones and reminder flow,
and reproduce the behavior. They remain included in the parser count; do not count them
again as a confirmed third cause without evidence.

## When reports arrived and when the desk merged them

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Arrival time and merge time are different events. A large merge batch is not evidence that
all those failures began that week. Review the late arrivals as well as the initial batch;
the desk continues to receive reports while this export stays fixed.

## Triage by cause, with evidence

The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) and
[accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) prescribe subject matching.
Their rendered counterexamples disagree with that rule. The layout marks the old review
date stale. Subject matching can merge different causes and split one cause across subjects.
Do not silently edit an accepted decision: propose a successor after review.

<DedupeExplorer />

<Callout>
Proposed replacement rule: resolve the tenant and entry route, reproduce or establish the
same cause, and only then merge. Different subjects can share a cause; matching subjects
can hide different causes. A missing tenant or uncertain symptom stays unclassified until
investigated. A fresh review date or a known status is not proof that a page is correct.
</Callout>

Keep each issue's count scoped to its export and keep its report IDs traceable. Recompute
when new reports arrive. Check this page's rendered tables, examples and signature before
the desk relies on it; the source prose alone does not show what its components will say.

